    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Transpression (i.e., a combination of strike-slip shear and shortening perpendicular to the shear zone) has been identified recently in many areas that were thought to have undergone either pure shortening or simple shear deformation. Where shortening is the dominant deformation mode, a component of strike-slip shear is recognized primarily by significant extension parallel to fold axes. In strike-slip fault systems transpressional segments can be recognized by uplift and the development of compressional features such as flower structures [Harding and Lowell, 1979]. The component of shortening is postulated to be dependent on the angle between the strike of the fault and the plate vector determined by relative plate motion calculations.\r\n\r\nIn this study, the strain field of a segment of the southern San Andreas fault (SAF) at Durmid Hill (Figure 1) is quantified and compared with the theoretical strain field of a transpressional shear zone. Shortening perpendicular to fold axes, extension parallel to fold axes, orientation of fold axes, and estimated vertical uplift rates and magnitudes are used as strain indicators. The Durmid Hill area offers a unique example of deformation of layered, soft lacustrine sediments near a major strike-slip fault, reminiscent of a macroscale clay model.\r\n\r\nEn echelon anticlines in transpressional settings can form important hydrocarbon traps along transform plate boundaries (e.g., California or Indonesia). A better understanding of the formation and underlying mechanics of these and other strike-slip related structures will therefore be of great importance.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
